q2/build.sh applies this to iroh 1.5.0's source before it builds q2-iroh (patch -p1, in a copy of the crate).

iroh runs a net report on a timer, every 20 to 26 s at random: QUIC address discovery to every relay
region it knows (n0's four), each a fresh QUIC handshake. On the Q2's MIPS that costs about 390 ms
of CPU a report: measured 2026-11-20 on V1KXi, q2-iroh's ticks second by second were 0 between
bursts of 39 about every 23 s, nearly all of its idle 2% of a core. With this the timer runs every
300 to 330 s instead. Untouched: a report at once on a link change (netwatch: Wi-Fi lost or joined,
an address changed), the relay connection's own 15 s ping, a connection's 5 s QUIC heartbeat, and
pkarr's 5 min republish. The trade-off: a public address or NAT mapping that changes without a
link event (rare on a home network) is noticed up to 5 min later instead of within 26 s, and the
NAT mapping the periodic report kept open while nothing is connected may expire, so a phone's
first connect after a long idle starts on the relay and moves to a direct path once hole punching
opens one, as it does from any new network.

